# NUMERICS: division and modulo signs.
#
# `/` on two integers truncates toward zero. `%` is FLOORED — the remainder
# takes the DIVISOR's sign — which is Lua's law and, since 2026-08-15, Idol's:
# see `docs/rulings.md` § "Modulo and floor division".
#
# THIS HEADER USED TO CITE `§12 B-4` for the truncating `%`, and **§12 B-4 does
# not exist in either tree.** It also used to say "It diverges from Lua, which
# floors" — a divergence no authority document ever licensed. `law.md`'s first
# line puts "ordinary Lua meaning" at the entry of the specialization chain, so
# the divergence was the thing needing a warrant, and it did not have one.
#
# THE `/` COLUMNS ARE DELIBERATELY UNMOVED. `-3 -3` is what they were and what
# they are. Lua's `/` is FLOAT division (`-7 / 2` is `-3.5`), so unlike `%` there
# is a real descriptor question here — a relation declared `: i64` has no float
# to return — and `docs/rulings.md` carries it as an OPEN ROW with its own
# `law:`/`today:`/`delta:`. Half a numeric law is worse than none, so it is
# stated as open rather than settled by momentum.
# expect: -3 -3 1 -1
main: i64 = ()
  a = -7 / 2
  b = 7 / -2
  c = -7 % 2
  d = 7 % -2
  print("{a} {b} {c} {d}")
  0
